 ## Overview
-Standard Constant Step Ray Marching is an iterative algorithm for finding the intersection of a ray with an implicit surface or sampling a volume by advancing the ray position at fixed intervals [S4, S5, unofficial]. It mitigates the drawback of root-finding methods that may identify multiple intersection points by focusing on determining the first root (or accumulating volume data) [S4, unofficial]. Unlike Spheretracing, which uses the distance field value to determine the step size, this method uses a predefined constant step size, allowing it to work with any function that returns a boolean (inside/outside) or a scalar field, rather than requiring a strict Signed Distance Field (SDF) [S2, S4, unofficial].
+Standard Constant Step Ray Marching is an iterative algorithm for finding the intersection of a ray with an implicit surface or sampling a volume by advancing the ray position at fixed intervals [S4, S5, unofficial]. It mitigates the drawback of root-finding methods that may identify multiple intersection points by focusing on determining the first root (or accumulating volume data) [S4, unofficial]. Unlike Spheretracing, which uses the distance field value to determine the step size, this method uses a predefined constant step size, allowing it to work with any function that returns a boolean (inside/outside) or a scalar field, rather than requiring a strict Signed Distance Field (SDF) [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray-primitive intersection](769-ray-primitive-intersection.md).
 
